WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Module review · Legal, Compliance, Policies & Risk
Recap: Legal, Compliance, Policies & Risk

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
Before you launch, check that your legal duties, policies and risks each have an owner.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:32.578
You learned what your legal duties are, which policies and procedures a new initiative actually needs and what each one is for, with templates to adapt to your local laws and have reviewed by a qualified lawyer. Then you built a risk register with owners, actions and escalation.

4
00:00:32.978 --> 00:00:33.578
One lesson at a time

5
00:00:33.578 --> 00:00:44.067
10.1 Your legal and regulatory map: Your legal duties come from your form, activities and locations, and from your first funders, customers or partners.

6
00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:54.926
10.2 Working out which policies you actually need: A new organisation needs fewer policies than you might think, and copied policies nobody follows create risks.

7
00:00:54.926 --> 00:01:05.674
10.3 Contracts and agreements: You will sign contracts before you are fully set up, so a register and pre-signature checks protect you.

8
00:01:06.074 --> 00:01:06.674
One lesson at a time

9
00:01:06.674 --> 00:01:17.533
10.4 Core policies: conduct, conflicts and anti-fraud: A small core of conduct, conflict of interest and anti-fraud policies is easiest to write before habits form.

10
00:01:17.533 --> 00:01:26.170
10.5 Safeguarding: Safeguarding applies from your very first activity or pilot, including online and in the stories you share.

11
00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:37.289
10.6 Data protection and privacy: Data protection applies from the first sign-up: collect only what you need, keep it secure and be open.

12
00:01:37.689 --> 00:01:38.289
One lesson at a time

13
00:01:38.289 --> 00:01:48.778
10.7 Whistleblowing, complaints and incident reporting: In a small team, a safe route to raise concerns with someone outside the founders is especially important.

14
00:01:48.778 --> 00:01:57.785
10.8 Writing usable procedures: Every important policy needs a usable procedure, even if only you carry out the task today.

15
00:01:57.785 --> 00:02:08.163
10.9 Policy management and review: Policies only protect people when they are approved, known, followed and reviewed through a simple cycle.

16
00:02:08.563 --> 00:02:09.163
One lesson at a time

17
00:02:09.163 --> 00:02:19.281
10.10 Identifying and assessing risk: Describing each risk as cause, event and consequence lets your response target the cause or soften the impact.

18
00:02:19.281 --> 00:02:29.400
10.11 Risk registers, ownership, mitigation and escalation: A short risk register with owners, actions and escalation rules is one you will actually use.

19
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:39.407
10.12 Monitoring and reviewing risk: Early risks change quickly, so regular reviews and early warning signs keep the register alive.

20
00:02:40.007 --> 00:02:52.607
Every foundation is now in place. Bring it all together, retake your health check against your starting point and review your foundations regularly as your initiative grows.

21
00:02:53.207 --> 00:02:58.007
You check your Compliance & Risk Pack and download each exercise as a PDF.

22
00:02:59.507 --> 00:03:02.607
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
